## Test Smell Audit — `CatalogTest.java`

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 4           | 4              |
| Medium   | 4           | 4              |
| Low      | 2           | 2              |
| **Total**| **10**      | **10**         |

*(1 test is clean — `addThrowsOnNegativeCount`)*

---

### HIGH Severity

#### 1. Conditional Test Logic — `addConditionalLogic` (line 26)

The `if (sku.equals("b"))` branch inside the loop changes setup behavior. A reader can't tell at a glance what quantities were added without mentally executing the branch.

```java
// Problem (line 29-33)
if (sku.equals("b")) {
    catalog.add(sku, 10);
} else {
    catalog.add(sku, 1);
}
```

**Fix:** Replace the branching loop with explicit, linear setup calls:

```java
@Test
void addConditionalLogic() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    catalog.add("b", 10);
    catalog.add("c", 1);
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
    assertEquals(10, catalog.quantity("b"));
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("c"));
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Some branch may never execute under future changes; intent is obscured.

---

#### 2. Mystery Guest — `addLoadsFromFile` (line 39)

The test creates, writes, and reads a real temp file via `java.nio.file.Files`. This couples the test to the filesystem and can fail for I/O reasons unrelated to `Catalog`.

```java
// Problem (lines 41-43)
Path tmp = Files.createTempFile("catalog", ".txt");
Files.writeString(tmp, "x:7");
String[] parts = Files.readString(tmp).split(":");
```

**Fix:** Inline the data — the file round-trip tests nothing about `Catalog`:

```java
@Test
void addLoadsFromFile() {
    catalog.add("x", 7);
    assertTrue(catalog.inStock("x"));
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky failures on restricted filesystems, CI sandboxes, or temp-dir quota limits.

---

#### 3. Sleepy Test — `addSleepy` (line 49)

`Thread.sleep(200)` adds 200 ms of dead time. `Catalog.add` is synchronous — there is nothing to wait for.

```java
// Problem (line 52)
Thread.sleep(200);
```

**Fix:** Remove the sleep entirely:

```java
@Test
void addSleepy() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slows the suite; scales badly as tests multiply; non-deterministic on slow CI runners.

---

#### 4. Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test) — `addAndDoNothing` (line 57)

The method calls `catalog.add` but never asserts anything. It will pass even if `add` silently drops the item.

```java
// Problem (lines 58-60)
@Test
void addAndDoNothing() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
}
```

**Fix:** Assert the expected postcondition:

```java
@Test
void addStoresQuantity() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — the test inflates coverage without verifying any behavior.

---

### MEDIUM Severity

#### 5. Eager Test — `doEverythingAtOnce` (line 63)

Calls 4 distinct production methods (`add`, `quantity`, `inStock`, `toString`) in a single test. When it fails, the root cause is ambiguous.

```java
// Problem (lines 65-72)
catalog.add("a", 1); catalog.add("b", 2); catalog.add("c", 3);
catalog.quantity("a"); catalog.quantity("b");
catalog.inStock("c");
catalog.toString();
assertEquals(6, catalog.quantity("a") + catalog.quantity("b") + catalog.quantity("c"));
```

**Fix:** Split into focused tests, one behavior each:

```java
@Test
void addAccumulatesQuantity() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    catalog.add("b", 2);
    catalog.add("c", 3);
    assertEquals(6, catalog.quantity("a") + catalog.quantity("b") + catalog.quantity("c"));
}
```

(Test `inStock` and `toString` separately.)

**Risk if unfixed:** A single failure is hard to diagnose; the test name doesn't communicate what it verifies.

